From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 05:44:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 098/190] segacd: say which kind of timeout, and put the log
 back on B:

The first log to come off a console read `why 1111` in all six rounds:
twenty-four attempts, every one a timeout, and not a single 2, 3 or 5
between them. That acquits everything this driver was built to
survive -- the head being past the target, the transfer failing, the
decoder mispairing a header against a pointer. Nothing arrived at all.

But nibble 1 covers two different faults that need opposite fixes. The
decoder may have produced nothing, which means the read never reached
the drive; or it may have produced sectors that never got as far as
the one wanted, which means the drive is reading in the wrong place.
cd_why cannot tell them apart and neither could I. So each timeout now
photographs the two numbers that can: how many sectors the decoder
announced during the attempt, and how far the newest one got relative
to the target. Taken before the next attempt resets the ring, and only
for a timeout, since the other verdicts already name themselves.
cdc_ints is a byte, so 65535 is an unambiguous "nothing has timed out".

The log itself comes back too. cdlog/DLOG were in patches/diag against
an older graft and never made it into this tree, which is why the
current build's diagnostics have been screen-only -- readable off a
television, one photograph at a time, six rounds of numbers that no
camera was going to carry. Every diagnostic line now goes to the
screen and to B:\CDLOG.TXT, rewritten whole each round so the file is
complete and closed at every moment the power might go off. From B:
the Mode 1 tool ROM lifts it into cart SRAM and the flash cart writes
it to an SD card. That round trip is proven: the log this commit is
reacting to came back with its checksum intact.

Verified under gpgx: the file is written, and the new line reads
`timeout: int 65535 d 32767` on a run where every read succeeds.
---
 bios/bios.c   | 122 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-------
 bios/segacd.c |  47 ++++++++++++++++++-
 bios/segacd.h |   3 +-
 3 files changed, 155 insertions(+), 17 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/bios.c b/bios/bios.c
index 8134fe4..72276b0 100644
--- a/bios/bios.c
+++ b/bios/bios.c
@@ -63,6 +63,8 @@
 #include "amiga.h"
 #include "lisa.h"
 #include "segacd.h"
+#include <stdarg.h>
+#include "doprintf.h"
 #include "coldfire.h"
 #if WITH_CLI
 #include "../cli/clistub.h"
@@ -806,6 +808,76 @@ BOOL can_shutdown(void)
  * exec the user interface (shell or AES)
  */
 
+#ifdef MACHINE_SEGACD
+/* The diagnostic outgrew the tube, and now it does not need it.
+ *
+ * Ten lines a round, a round every few seconds, and the answer to
+ * "which sector does it die on" scrolls off a CRT that has no
+ * scroll-back. Worse, reading it off a television means transcribing
+ * it by hand, and the last hardware run produced six rounds of numbers
+ * that no photograph was going to carry.
+ *
+ * So every line goes to two places: the screen, and a buffer written
+ * to B:\CDLOG.TXT after each round. B: on a stock console is the 8 KB
+ * of internal backup RAM on the sub CPU's own bus -- the only
+ * persistent store the machine has, since a cartridge in the slot
+ * stops it booting from CD at all. EmuTOS will not touch that memory
+ * while it holds Sega-format game saves, so the log has nowhere to go
+ * until C is held at power-on once to claim it; that erases whatever
+ * is in there, which is why it is a deliberate act and not something
+ * this build does on its own.
+ *
+ * From there boot/m1tool.S lifts it into cart SRAM and the flash cart
+ * writes it to an SD card, so the round trip is complete without a
+ * camera. That path is proven: the first log it carried came back with
+ * its checksum intact.
+ *
+ * The buffer is written whole each round rather than appended to, so
+ * the file is complete and closed at every moment the power might go
+ * off. When it fills, it stops: the file stops changing and says so,
+ * and the screen keeps running. */
+#define CDLOG_MAX 3584
+
+static char cdlog[CDLOG_MAX];
+static UWORD cdlog_len;
+static UBYTE cdlog_full;
+
+static void cdlog_putc(int c)
+{
+    if (cdlog_len < CDLOG_MAX)
+        cdlog[cdlog_len++] = (char)c;
+    else
+        cdlog_full = 1;
+}
+
+PRINTF_STYLE static void cdlog_printf(const char *fmt, ...);
+static void cdlog_printf(const char *fmt, ...)
+{
+    va_list ap;
+
+    va_start(ap, fmt);
+    doprintf(cdlog_putc, fmt, ap);
+    va_end(ap);
+}
+
+/* Both at once. Every line the diagnostic prints is a line worth
+ * keeping, so there is no version of this where they can drift. */
+#define DLOG(...)                                                       \
+    do { cprintf(__VA_ARGS__); cdlog_printf(__VA_ARGS__); } while (0)
+
+static LONG cdlog_save(void)
+{
+    LONG h, rc;
+
+    h = Fcreate("B:\\CDLOG.TXT", 0);
+    if (h < 0L)
+        return h;
+    rc = Fwrite((WORD)h, (LONG)cdlog_len, cdlog);
+    Fclose((WORD)h);
+    return rc;
+}
+#endif
+
